<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Relax – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning to Balance</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Relax</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe the process of adapting to a new environment. Interactive: Facilitate peer feedback sessions on presentations.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe the process of balancing school and social activities, explaining strategies to manage time and reduce stress (e.g., "I balance my schoolwork and social life by planning my study time around social events and using a calendar to stay organized. This helps me avoid feeling overwhelmed.")
            Can analyze persuasive pieces about managing relaxation effectively and provide constructive feedback during peer sessions (e.g., "The article argues that students who set clear boundaries between school and personal time tend to have lower stress levels.")
            Can discuss the benefits of balancing school work and relaxation (e.g., "The story suggests that having time for both schoolwork and relaxation helps students stay focused.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of students engaged in various activities: studying intensely, practicing a hobby (e.g., painting, playing sports), and socializing with friends. Students work in pairs to discuss which images represent "balance" and which suggest "stress" or "overwhelm". They share one strategy they have been practicing to manage their workload. (e.g., "I have been using a calendar to organize my study schedule and social life.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target grammar and vocabulary. "To achieve a healthy <b>balance</b>, you <b>might</b> need to <b>prioritize</b> certain <b>activities</b>. For example, I have been making sure to allocate specific <b>downtime</b> for myself." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /ɪ/ in 'activities' and /iː/ in 'relieve' by contrasting them: "Balancing school <b>activities</b> can help <b>relieve</b> stress." Teacher also models giving suggestions: "You <b>should</b> plan your day better to avoid <b>procrastination</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I have been studying late every night,' am I still studying now or finished?" (Expected answer: Still studying, ongoing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the difference in sound between 'live' (verb) and 'leave' (verb)?" (Expected answer: Short /ɪ/ vs. long /iː/.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Balance")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio interview (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) with two 12th-grade students discussing their strategies for managing schoolwork, social life, and relaxation. The dialogue will naturally feature present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals of possibility, along with target vocabulary (e.g., <b>schedule</b>, <b>boundaries</b>, <b>mindfulness</b>, <b>workload</b>, <b>wellbeing</b>). Register is informal, conversational.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> To interact orally in various situations for social purposes, exchanging information and expressing ideas, opinions, feelings and emotions appropriately and coherently.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Speaking focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Pair Discussion):</b> After the first listening, students discuss with a partner: "What are the main challenges the students in the interview have been facing regarding their balance?" and "What is one new strategy for <b>relaxation</b> or <b>time management</b> mentioned?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Group Share):</b> After a second listening, students receive a list of incomplete sentences (e.g., "If Maria had more free time, she ______." "Carlos has been ______ to avoid burnout.") and orally complete them with information from the interview, then share their answers with their small group.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice describing ongoing efforts using present perfect continuous, expressing hypothetical situations with the second conditional, and giving suggestions using modals of possibility. They will integrate target vocabulary (e.g., <b>prioritize</b>, <b>manage</b>, <b>flexibility</b>, <b>mindfulness</b>, <b>wellbeing</b>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Builder (Individual & Choral Speaking):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board, focusing on the target grammar. For example: "I have been trying to ______ (verb + -ing) by ______." "If I had more ______, I would ______." "You <b>might</b> ______ to ______." Students individually complete these orally using target vocabulary (e.g., "I have been trying to <b>manage</b> my <b>workload</b> by setting clear <b>boundaries</b>."). The teacher then leads a choral repetition focusing on pronunciation of key words like 'activities' /ækˈtɪvɪtɪz/ and 'relieve' /rɪˈliːv/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Chain Drill (Group Speaking):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student starts a sentence, e.g., "To avoid <b>stress</b>, I have been..." The next student continues the sentence with a different strategy or adds to it, using the present perfect continuous and new vocabulary. The third student then offers a suggestion using a modal verb (e.g., "You <b>might</b> try <b>mindfulness</b>."). The fourth student then uses a second conditional to respond (e.g., "If I had more discipline, I <b>would</b> certainly do that."). This continues around the circle.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Advice Column" (Pair Speaking):</b> In pairs, students take turns presenting a personal challenge related to balancing school and social life (e.g., "I have been feeling overwhelmed trying to keep up with school <b>activities</b> and still have a <b>social life</b>."). Their partner must respond by giving at least two suggestions using modals for possibility or should (e.g., "You <b>should</b> consider blocking out time for <b>downtime</b> in your <b>schedule</b>. You <b>might</b> also try to <b>prioritize</b> your tasks each day."). They then swap roles.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Balance")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Peer Feedback Session. Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to discuss a provided case study (a fictional 12th-grade student struggling with balance). Each group must collaboratively analyze the situation and present their suggested strategies to the class. Following each presentation, groups will facilitate a brief peer feedback session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Case Study Analysis (Group Discussion):</b> The teacher provides a written case study of a student, "Daniel," who is struggling to balance his demanding school <b>workload</b>, part-time job, and desire for a social life, leading to high <b>stress</b> levels and lack of <b>relaxation</b>. Groups discuss Daniel's situation, using the present perfect continuous to describe his ongoing struggles (e.g., "Daniel has been taking on too many <b>activities</b>, and he has been neglecting his <b>wellbeing</b>."), second conditional to propose hypothetical solutions (e.g., "If Daniel had a clearer <b>schedule</b>, he <b>would</b> feel less overwhelmed."), and modals for possibility to suggest concrete strategies (e.g., "He <b>might</b> need to set clearer <b>boundaries</b> for his work hours."). Groups brainstorm a list of advice. (Focus: Sociolinguistic Competence - leading collaborative projects, encouraging inclusive communication, sharing cultural perspectives and strategies).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Presentation:</b> Each group orally presents their analysis of Daniel's situation and their proposed solutions to the class. They must clearly explain the process of adapting to a new routine and suggest specific strategies to manage time and reduce stress, incorporating the target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Feedback Session:</b> After each group's presentation, the presenting group facilitates a brief Q&A and feedback session (2-3 minutes) with the other groups. Peers provide constructive feedback using pragmatic functions like "I think your suggestion about ______ is very effective. You <b>could</b> also consider ______." or "The article argues that students who set clear <b>boundaries</b> tend to have lower stress levels." This actively involves students in giving suggestions and expressing opinions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking fluency, accuracy of target grammar and vocabulary, and effective participation in peer feedback. Peer assessment sheet for feedback received during presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' oral group presentations and their participation in the peer feedback sessions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, specifically distinguishing /ɪ/ in "activities" and /iː/ in "relieve" when relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and varied use of nouns, verbs, and adjectives related to balance and relaxation (e.g., <b>mindfulness</b>, <b>prioritize</b>, <b>flexible</b>, <b>downtime</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Ideas presented are clear, logical, and easy to follow. Effective use of present perfect continuous, second conditional, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and Interaction:</b> Active participation in group discussion and constructive contribution to peer feedback sessions (giving suggestions, expressing opinions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new strategy for managing <b>stress</b> or improving <b>wellbeing</b> that you learned or discussed today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel using modals like 'might' or 'could' to give suggestions?" (Scale of 1-5).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Based on today's discussion, what is one concrete change you could make to better <b>balance</b> your school <b>workload</b> and <b>relaxation</b> time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did giving and receiving feedback help you understand the topic of 'Learning to Balance' more deeply?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group discussions on the case study showed high engagement and good use of target vocabulary and grammar, especially the modals for suggestion. Students were able to facilitate peer feedback effectively. I noticed some students still struggled with consistent use of the present perfect continuous and distinguishing /ɪ/ and /iː/ in rapid speech. I will need to provide more explicit short drills and perhaps a quick mini-lesson on specific contexts for these sounds in the next lesson's warm-up.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4, students will apply their understanding and speaking practice to a writing task. They will compose a short essay or reflective journal entry about their personal strategies for achieving balance and relaxation, incorporating the grammar structures and vocabulary practiced today. This will allow them to consolidate their ideas and practice expressing them in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>